<section class="w3-methods" id="methods" aria-labelledby="w3-methods-title">
  <div class="w3-methods-inner">
    <header class="w3-methods-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="w3-methods-kicker"><span></span> INSIDE THE INSPECTION</p>
        <h2 id="w3-methods-title">Follow the evidence.<br /><em>Choose the right test.</em></h2>
      </div>
      <p>No single device finds every leak. The checks depend on the clues, the plumbing, and what the technician can access.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="w3-methods-board">
      <article class="w3-method-feature">
        <figure class="w3-method-photo w3-method-photo-feature">
          <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/32497162/pexels-photo-32497162.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1500" alt="Technician checking a bathroom with an inspection device" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption><span>FIELD CHECK 01</span><span>LISTEN FOR THE SOURCE</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="w3-method-copy w3-method-copy-feature">
          <p class="w3-method-eyebrow">SOUND · HIDDEN WATER LINES</p>
          <h3>Acoustic listening</h3>
          <p>A contact sensor listens for sound vibrations from water escaping a pressurized pipe. It can help narrow the search under floors or behind finished surfaces.</p>
          <details class="w3-method-more"><summary>What affects the reading <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Noise from appliances, traffic, or other plumbing—and the pipe material—can change what the sensor picks up. The sound is one clue, interpreted alongside the rest of the inspection.</p></details>
        </div>
      </article>

      <div class="w3-method-side">
        <article class="w3-method-secondary">
          <figure class="w3-method-photo">
            <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/7859781/pexels-photo-7859781.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1000" alt="Technician holding a thermal imaging camera" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption><span>02 · TEMPERATURE</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="w3-method-copy">
            <p class="w3-method-eyebrow">SURFACE PATTERNS</p>
            <h3>Thermal imaging</h3>
            <p>An infrared camera shows temperature differences on a surface. Hot or cold water may leave a pattern worth checking further.</p>
            <details class="w3-method-more"><summary>What it can tell you <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>It does not see through walls or confirm a leak on its own. Surface materials, airflow, and room conditions can affect the image.</p></details>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="w3-method-secondary">
          <figure class="w3-method-photo">
            <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/7937300/pexels-photo-7937300.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1000" alt="Pressure gauge fitted to a network of metal pipes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption><span>03 · LINE TEST</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="w3-method-copy">
            <p class="w3-method-eyebrow">CHECK THE SUPPLY</p>
            <h3>Pressure checks</h3>
            <p>A gauge checks whether an isolated water line holds pressure. A change can point to water loss in that section.</p>
            <details class="w3-method-more"><summary>What it can tell you <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>A pressure change does not pinpoint the leak by itself. The technician uses the result with the reported symptoms and other checks.</p></details>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="w3-methods-footnote"><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span> The method should fit the problem. Not every inspection needs every tool.</p>
  </div>
</section>

